      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The island’s first European name was Little Barent Island, named after Danish cowherd Barent Jansen Blom, while Wards Island was called Great Barent Island.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Randalls Island Park was created in 1936 and originally centered around the Triborough Bridge's T‑shaped viaduct.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        23
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Randall's Island Sports Foundation, founded in 1992, runs free youth programs and workshops across the park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Randalls_and_Wards_Islands",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Randalls and Wards Islands sit at the confluence of the Harlem River, East River, and Bronx Kill, making it one of New York City’s most strategically located spots.",
